  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1090,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1106,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154202,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1106,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable: </em><em>DICER1</em> syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1091,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1107,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154203,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1107,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1092,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1108,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154204,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1108,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the gynaecological tract, tumours typically arise in the ovary and uterine cervix. In patients with DICER1 syndrome, bilateral ovarian tumours may occasionally occur {32333409}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1093,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1109,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154205,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1109,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Affected individuals are predominantly children and young adults. Approximately 5% of carriers develop their first neoplasm before the age of 10&nbsp;years (female carriers: 4%; male carriers: 7%). The average incidence of neoplasms by the age of 50&nbsp;years is 19%, with a substantially higher risk in female individuals (female individuals: 27%; male individuals: 10%). Gynaecological neoplasms predominate in older patients {30715996}. Key clinical phenotypes associated with germline <em>DICER1</em> pathogenic variants are presented in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/85/291/53065\" target=\"_blank\">#53065</a>Table&nbsp;17.XXX.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1094,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1110,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154206,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1110,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The overall incidence is unknown. However, in one population-scale study, putative loss-of-function variants in <em>DICER1</em> were present in 1&nbsp;in 3700&nbsp;individuals to 1&nbsp;in 4600&nbsp;individuals {33630087}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1095,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1111,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154207,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1111,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>DICER1</em>-related tumour predisposition syndrome is caused by germline pathogenic variants in <em>DICER1</em> {19556464}, which may also arise de&nbsp;novo or cause somatic mosaicism {26475046}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1096,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1112,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154208,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1112,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>For tumour development to occur in patients with <em>DICER1</em>-related tumour predisposition syndrome, heterozygous germline loss-of-function variants is typically accompanied by a somatic missense mutation in the other <em>DICER1</em> allele. These typically affect the RNase&nbsp;IIIb domain (compound <em>DICER1 </em>mutations) {22187960}. DICER1 is an RNase&nbsp;III endoribonuclease and is essential for processing microRNAs in the RNA interference pathway. Somatic missense variants in the RNase&nbsp;IIIb domain of DICER1 disrupt processing of the hairpin-shaped precursor microRNAs, resulting in an excess of 3p microRNAs and a deficiency of 5p microRNAs, leading to aberrant posttranscriptional regulation of multiple cancer gene networks {25176334}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1103,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1119,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154209,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1119,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance of DICER1-related tumour predisposition syndrome&ndash;associated neoplasms is nonspecific.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1097,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1113,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154210,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1113,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumours in patients with <em>DICER1</em> germline variants are often moderately to poorly differentiated, or retiform and may display heterologous elements, such as mucinous epithelium and fetal-type cartilage and/or skeletal muscle differentiation {28654427; 29037807; 38657450} (<em>see </em><a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/167\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumour</em>). Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma of the female genital tract is commonly associated with <em>DICER1</em> syndrome and most often arises in the uterine cervix. Tumours often display fetal-type cartilage {35941719}, a feature highly characteristic of <em>DICER1</em> association {33846547} (<em>see </em><a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/227\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>R</em><em>habdomyosarcoma</em>). Histologically, both <em>DICER1</em>-associated Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumours and embryonal rhabdomyosarcomas are indistinguishable from their non-syndromic counterparts {28654427; 22187960; 31900434}. Other rare tumours in the female genital tract associated with <em>DICER1</em> syndrome include gynandroblastoma (<em>see </em><a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/169\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>G</em><em>ynandroblastoma</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1098,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1114,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154211,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1114,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1099,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1115,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154212,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1115,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A germline <em>DICER1</em> pathogenic variant must be confirmed.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1102,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1118,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154213,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1118,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> germline <em>DICER1</em> pathogenic variant.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1100,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1116,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154214,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1116,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging is the same as for other neoplasms at the corresponding sites.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1101,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 3,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1117,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154215,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 1117,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Outcome in patients with Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumours and embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma of the female genital tract is generally favourable; however, certain features in Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumours may be associated with adverse outcomes (<em>see </em><a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/167\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumour</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T11:34:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]